  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA surface cold front will be passing through the region from\nwest to east this evening. With limited moisture in place, no\nprecipitation is expected with the crossing of this boundary,\nwith just a band of clouds expected. Behind this boundary, winds\nwill switch to the north for tonight into Tuesday. There may\nbriefly be a gusty breeze with the passage of the boundary into\ntonight, but winds will decrease for Tuesday, as high pressure\nover Ontario builds towards the area. This will keep dry weather\nin place with a mostly sunny sky for Tuesday. Temperatures will\nbe noticeably cooler on Tuesday, with highs only in the upper \n40s to upper 50s across the region with dewpoint values in the \n20s. With the high pressure nearly overhead, good radiational\ncooling is expected on Tuesday night with clear skies and calm\nwinds. Have lowered temps below the NBM mean, with widespread\nmid to upper 20s for min temps for Tuesday night into Wednesday\nmorning. With the dry and cold air mass in place, the only fog\nthat develops will likely be over the immediate waterways. \n\nAs the high pressure drifts eastward, sunny and dry weather \nwill continue into Wednesday. Temps will be a few degrees warmer\ncompared to Tuesday, but most places will see highs in the 50s,\nwhich is close to normal for late October.  \n\nTemperatures will turn a little milder for Thursday, with valley\nhighs back into the 60s with plenty of sunshine. Another cold\nfront looks to cross for Thursday night into Friday. Some\nshowers could accompany this boundary across the Adirondacks,\nbut overall moisture is still limited and forcing isn't the best\neither. Most places will continue to remain dry. While it should\nbe mild again into Friday, temperatures will return closer to\nnormal for the upcoming weekend. As of right now, no widespread\nprecipitation is expected through Saturday night. Some models\nshow the potential for some rainfall for Sunday into Monday, but\nthis is still far out enough to keep the forecast rather\nuncertain.",
